{"id": "swebq0158", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 2.55, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1726, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "Constitutional republic;Presidential system;Republic;Unitary state", "query": "what form of government do we have in the philippines?", "transcript": "What form of government do we have in the Philippines?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The Philippines has a presidential system of government. The president is both the head of state and head of government, elected by popular vote for a six-year term. Legislative power is held by a bicameral Congress, consisting of the Senate and the House of Representatives. The judicial branch is independent and includes the Supreme Court. This structure is outlined in the 1987 Philippine Constitution, which emphasizes democratic principles, the rule of law, and protection of human rights.", "answer_ms": 3129}
